  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers and thunderstorms remain possible \ntoday and Friday.\n\nMonitoring a cluster of showers with embedded thunderstorms south of \nI-90 early this morning, mainly impacting Connecticut as of 0530Z. \nWhile storms remain sub-severe, they are producing lightning and \nlocalized wind gusts up to 40 mph. This complex of showers and \nstorms will move east toward Rhode Island and southeastern \nMassachusetts prior to sunrise, leading to a wet start for the early \nmorning commute. However, multiple runs of the HRRR overnight have \nshown the potential for a secondary cluster of showers and embedded \nthunderstorms to redevelop across eastern Connecticut, Rhode Island, \nand southeastern Massachusetts from 09Z to 12Z. Otherwise, expect a \ncloudy start to the day.\n\nClouds disperse this afternoon with daytime heating and mixing \nwithin the boundary layer, allowing temperatures to warm into the \nlower to middle 80s. Storm coverage this afternoon remains limited. \nAs mentioned by the previous forecaster, the mid-levels are dry with \nnorthwest flow. Outside of a few hit-or-miss showers, the afternoon \nis largely dry as the better forcing remains associated with the mid-\nlevel trough and shortwave across northern New England. By late this \nevening, the first cold front moves offshore, with a secondary surge \nof drier air reinforcing on Friday. CAMs try to initiate a few pop-\nup storms along the edge of this boundary, but given the dry air in \nplace, don't expect anything more than isolated coverage of garden-\nvariety thunderstorms. The vast majority of southern New England \nremains dry, with lower humidity and highs in the lower 80s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry, seasonable, and comfortable weather returns for \nthe weekend.\n\nQuiet weather-wise this weekend as an area of high pressure \noriginating from northern Canada settles across the Northeast for \nSaturday and the first half of Sunday. PWATs lower quite \ndramatically, with values between 0.5 and 0.7 inches, or nearly one \nstandard deviation below normal. Thus, little to no humidity is \nexpected Saturday, and conditions may actually feel quite dry, with \nalmost fall-like humidity. Temperatures remain near seasonable \nlevels, ranging from the upper 70s to lower 80s. Saturday night into \nSunday morning, clear skies and light winds provide favorable \nconditions for effective radiational cooling. Lows tumble into the \nmid-50s, while urban centers hover near 60 degrees. On Sunday, high \npressure exits as a low pressure system approaches from the Great \nLakes region. Warm air advection ushers moisture northward from the \nsouthwest with the passage of a warm front, resulting in increasing \ncloud cover through the afternoon. Temperatures remain seasonable \nand similar to Saturday, though dew points increase to near 60 \ndegrees.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Rain chances return late Sunday into Monday, \nfollowed by quieter weather into the middle of next week.\n\nThe next opportunity for rainfall comes during the second half of \nSunday into Monday. However, there remains uncertainty regarding \nrainfall amounts, as the best forcing is associated with the surface \nlow and track variability makes it difficult to pin down where the \ngreatest rainfall will occur. This is worth monitoring, as global \nguidance is in agreement that PWATs may exceed 2 inches, indicating \nthe potential for heavy rainfall. This is also supported by a \nMarginal Risk (level 1 of 4) for excessive rainfall. Global \nensembles highlight areas north of I-90 as having the greatest \nlikelihood for measurable rainfall, with a 40-60 percent probability \nof at least a half inch of rain between Sunday evening and Monday \nevening. That said, if rainfall becomes more convective, localized \ntotals could be higher. One positive is that this system appears \nprogressive, with the cold front clearing east of New England on \nMonday. Drier air then advects in from the northwest, leading to \nanother dry stretch from the latter half of Monday through Tuesday \nand Wednesday as surface high pressure builds back into the region. \nDuring this time, temperatures are expected to remain seasonable, \nwith highs in the lower to middle 80s and tolerable humidity as dew \npoints remain in the lower 60s.",
